ems:\n\n1. Heterogeneous quantum error correction. Suppose the nodes of the network use different quantum error-correcting codes (different code families, code distances, or physical encodings), so that an entangled pair shared across a link may be encoded in code C_A at one end and code C_B at the other. Construct quantum error-correction protocols that enable reliable storage and transmission of entanglement across such heterogeneously encoded links, including explicit encoding/decoding maps that interconvert between codes while preserving entanglement fidelity, and prove performance guarantees (threshold conditions, achievable logical fidelities, resource overhead) under realistic local and channel noise models.\n\n2. Robust entanglement purification. Given many copies of a noisy bipartite (or multipartite) entangled state ρ shared between network nodes, design entanglement purification protocols that distill a smaller number of higher-fidelity entangled states, and that remain effective in the presence of memory decoherence during the protocol, gate noise, measurement errors, and classical communication delays. Quantify the achievable yield-fidelity trade-offs and prove robustness bounds for such protocols under composite noise models incorporating these imperfections.\n\n3. Entanglement access control. When several pairs or groups of nodes simultaneously request access to a shared multipartite entangled resource (for example, a GHZ state or graph state shared among many parties), design entanglement access-control mechanisms that resolve contention. Such a mechanism must specify how the multipartite resource is allocated, manipulated (e.g., by local measurements that project onto bipartite or smaller multipartite sub-resources), and scheduled among requests, with provable fairness, throughput, and fidelity-preservation properties.\n\nProvide protocols solving all three subproblems and rigorous analytic guarantees on their performance, or establish fundamental limits showing what cannot be achieved.","status_search_result":"The three subproblems—heterogeneous quantum error correction across network links, decoherence-robust entanglement purification, and entanglement access control for multipartite resources—are active research directions in quantum networking. While individual schemes (heterogeneous Bell-pair encoding, various purification protocols, initial entanglement access-control proposals) have appeared, no comprehensive solution with general performance guarantees exists, and the questions are widely described as open challenges in quantum link-layer design.","status_evidence":[{"title":"Heterogeneously encoded Bell pairs and link-layer QEC","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04492","snippet":"Ongoing research is developing QEC protocols for heterogeneous networks and purification protocols robust to decoherence, noise and other errors; entanglement access control is being developed to resolve contention over multipartite resources.","claim":"These topics are presented as ongoing, unresolved research directions for the quantum link layer."}]},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"c155a33f-424b-4ce6-9945-30ea4a066ca4"}
